<Base title="Sources and method" description="How Humans on Mars checks facts, labels plans and scenarios, and which sources the tools use.">
  <section class="prose-wrap">
    <article class="prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">Method</p>
      <h1 class="display-sm">Sources and method</h1>
      <h2>Four editorial states</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Label</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><span class="label-status">Fact</span></td><td>Backed by published evidence, with the source linked.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><span class="label-status">Plan</span></td><td>An intention announced by an organisation, attributed with its date. Not a demonstrated capability.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><span class="label-status">Model</span></td><td>A calculation whose inputs and assumptions are shown.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><span class="label-status">Scenario</span></td><td>An imagined scene to help you picture daily life, labelled as creative.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <h2>How we check</h2>
      <p>
        We rely on NASA, JPL, ESA, mission teams, company primary material and peer-reviewed research. News reports and social posts
        are leads, not evidence. AI tools help with drafting and code review, but an AI answer is never cited as a source; a person
        opens the primary source before anything is published.
      </p>
      <h2>Sources used by the tools</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/mars/facts/">NASA: Mars facts</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www1.grc.nasa.gov/beginners-guide-to-aeronautics/weight-equation-2/">NASA Glenn: the weight equation</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/planets/approx_pos.html">NASA JPL: approximate positions of the planets</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.giss.nasa.gov/tools/mars24/help/algorithm.html">NASA GISS: Mars24 algorithm</a></li>
      </ul>
    </article>
  </section>
</Base>
